<article class="env-lemma" id="0A6I">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0A6I">Lemma <span data-tag="0A6I">47.3.8</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a Noetherian ring. Let $S \subset R$ be a multiplicative subset. If $E$ is an injective $R$-module, then $S^{-1}E$ is an injective $S^{-1}R$-module. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since $R \to S^{-1}R$ is an epimorphism of rings, it suffices to show that $S^{-1}E$ is injective as an $R$-module, see Lemma <a href="/tag/08YV" data-tag="08YV">47.3.3</a>. To show this we use Injectives, Lemma <a href="/tag/05NU" data-tag="05NU">19.2.6</a>. Thus let $I \subset R$ be an ideal and let $\varphi : I \to S^{-1} E$ be an $R$-module map. As $I$ is a finitely presented $R$-module (because $R$ is Noetherian) we can find an $f \in S$ and an $R$-module map $I \to E$ such that $f\varphi $ is the composition $I \to E \to S^{-1}E$ (Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0583" data-tag="0583">10.10.2</a>). Then we can extend $I \to E$ to a homomorphism $R \to E$. Then the composition 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  R \to E \to S^{-1}E \xrightarrow {f^{-1}} S^{-1}E  \]
</div>
    <p>
       is the desired extension of $\varphi $ to $R$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>